<section class="page-lead about-lead page-shell" aria-labelledby="about-title">
  <p class="eyebrow">About ForgeWire Labs</p>
  <h1 id="about-title">We build for the conditions <em>people actually have.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead-copy">A reliable system cannot assume unlimited compute, permanent connectivity, a particular model provider, or a person watching every step. ForgeWire Labs is independent engineering and research focused on the software surrounding intelligent work: execution, state, tools, permissions, evidence, communication, and the applications people use to direct it.</p>
</section>

<section class="organization-purpose page-shell" aria-labelledby="purpose-title">
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">Purpose</p>
    <h2 id="purpose-title">Why this work exists.</h2>
  </div>
  <div>
    <p>We began with agent-driven software and kept finding the same weakness: a strong model response doesn't make the surrounding system dependable. Context gets lost. Tools reach too far. Work crosses machines without enough accountability. A new contributor inherits code but not the decisions behind it.</p>
    <p>We build around those failures. Some solutions become capabilities in ForgeWire or Fabric. Others need to stand on their own, as RepoPact, Flink, Anvil, and SkillForge Academy do.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="about-principle" aria-labelledby="approach-title">
  <div class="page-shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">Our approach</p>
    <h2 id="approach-title">Use what works. Know what it does.</h2>
    <ul class="approach-list">
      <li><strong>Use what works.</strong> We adopt existing tools and libraries when they fit. We build our own components when requirements, ownership, operating cost, or integration tradeoffs justify it.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep control explicit.</strong> Models can propose actions; systems must decide what is authorized. People need meaningful ways to understand and intervene.</li>
      <li><strong>Make state survive the session.</strong> Project intent, decisions, work history, and evidence shouldn't disappear when a conversation ends or a tool changes.</li>
      <li><strong>Treat failure as normal.</strong> Machines disconnect. Providers change. Software breaks. We account for those conditions instead of hiding them behind a diagram.</li>
      <li><strong>Show the evidence.</strong> A release, a test, a prototype, and a research idea make different claims. We distinguish them and link to what supports each one.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Those choices matter more to us than a polished demo that cannot explain what happened when something fails.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="organization-practice page-shell" aria-labelledby="practice-title">
  <div class="organization-copy">
    <p class="eyebrow">Independent work, shared standards</p>
    <h2 id="practice-title">Separate products for different jobs.</h2>
    <p>Our projects solve different problems. They don't require the entire ForgeWire Labs ecosystem to be installed together, and we don't present unfinished integrations as shipped features. Where systems connect, we describe the boundary. Where they don't, they remain independent. We publish source, technical notes, and release information through the projects that own them.</p>
    <a class="text-link" href="{{ site.basePath }}/projects/">Explore our software <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
  </div>
</section>

